comments[3]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-08-07T06:07:15.705Z","harness:claude-code","The same class cost a second diagnosis today, and this occurrence proves the verdict half of this item is worse than described.\n\nMain CI failed at c91991001 on Coverage shard 2 of 4. The shard job log contained exactly two useful lines: the blob report was written, and the process exited 1. No test name, no assertion, no stack. The reason is that the shards run with the blob reporter, so all failure detail is serialized into the blob artifact and nothing is printed, and the merge job that reads the blobs declares needs on the shard matrix, so it is skipped precisely when a shard fails. The only job that can explain a shard failure never runs when there is a shard failure to explain.\n\nCost of the workaround. Reproducing the shard locally without the CI threshold overrides exited 1 for an unrelated reason, coverage thresholds evaluated per shard, which reads exactly like the CI failure and is not it. Reproducing with the exact CI argument vector exited 0. The failure was only identifiable by downloading the 18.5 MB blob artifact and resolving its index-referenced encoding by hand, where values are integer offsets into the same flat array, so state and errors are indices rather than data.\n\nWhat it actually was, and it is not this item's budget. tests/unit/sdk/workspace-snapshot.spec.ts, the test that renews the writer lease beyond its TTL, asserted that a competing lock acquisition rejects, and the promise resolved instead. The test writes a thousand files, starts a restore with a thirty millisecond lease, polls for the writer lock, sleeps eighty milliseconds, and then requires the competing acquire to be refused. That requires the restore to still be in flight and still renewing at that instant, which is a wall-clock assumption about a thousand-file copy on a shared runner. A rerun of the failed job alone passed, confirming a timing flake rather than a regression. The race is filed separately.\n\nConsequence for this item. The verdict half is not only that a test failure suppresses the coverage number. It is that a sharded failure suppresses the failure itself, so the gate contributors are told to trust reports neither the coverage nor the cause.\n"
  "2026-08-12T08:32:12.839Z","harness:codex","Implementation evidence: coverage mode now always runs the exact threshold gate after Vitest and reports independent verdicts with exit codes 0=both pass, 1=test only, 2=coverage only, 3=combined. Tests cover all verdicts and termination signals. The real wildcard install budget is 90 seconds in both twins and completed in about 21 seconds during the exact full run."
  "2026-08-12T09:05:14.793Z","harness:codex","Exact-head PR #990 evidence at bdd380af841efce5825bc27520e2d28463e2d527: all hosted executable checks passed, including Windows Node 24, four coverage shards plus aggregate coverage, static/typecheck/compat/smoke gates, Linux/macOS runtimes, CodeQL, Semgrep, Trivy, TruffleHog, Codecov patch, and 11 CodSpeed benchmarks. Fresh review inventory found zero inline threads and no actionable findings. CodeRabbit and Sourcery substantive reviews were rate-limited and Greptile produced no review surface; those are recorded limitations, not approvals. Every emitted bot comment/review was individually reacted to and answered with evidence."
notes[1]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-08-12T08:32:13.416Z","harness:codex","Observability contract: the exact coverage verdict must run even when tests fail, because a failing assertion and stale/missing coverage are independent defects. The workflow also keeps default assertion output while uploading blob reports for merge."
learnings[1]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-08-12T08:32:13.874Z","harness:codex","A wrapper that exits on the first child failure destroys diagnostic information. Aggregate independent gate results first, then return a stable combined exit contract so local agents and hosted CI see the same complete failure state."
